---
/* THE REVEALED BAND. One device, two consumers: each Plate on `/` and the
   Masthead portrait on every page but /about/ime/.

   IT IS A COMPONENT BECAUSE THE MASTHEAD LEFT THE INDEX. Both copies used to be
   markup in index.astro, styled by one set of rules deliberately written without
   a `.plate` ancestor so they reached both — and index.astro:619 called that
   "what makes 'one device seen twice' a fact about the stylesheet rather than a
   claim in a comment". Astro scopes by attribute, so the moment the Masthead
   became a component of its own those rules stopped reaching its band. The
   choice was to fork the device or to extract it. About.astro:143 had already
   named this exact trade and deferred it once, "worth revisiting if a third page
   ever opens this way"; six Project pages and a 404 are that third page.

   WHY NOT A STYLESHEET. docs/styling.md names three homes for CSS and then says
   "Do not invent a fourth location" — no band.css. It would also ship the band's
   geometry, its scrim and its motion to /about/ime/ and /404, which render no
   band at all, because everything imported in Base.astro lands on every page.

   WHAT THIS COSTS, STATED PLAINLY: the band's geometry depends on selectors it
   does not own — `.plate` and `.masthead-aside` — so those cross the scope
   boundary with `:global()` below. Two ancestor class names reaching in is a
   real debt, and it is much the smaller one: a fork is invisible, and nothing in
   CI can see it. check-css.mjs asserts refusals and token contrast; it has no
   duplication check and cannot acquire one.

   THE :global() FORM IS THE WHOLE COMPOUND — `:global(.plate:hover)`, never
   `:global(.plate):hover`. Same shape as [slug].astro's
   `:global(html:has(dialog.expanded[open]))`. */
interface Props {
  /** 0 for the About band, 1..n for a Plate. Padded to two digits here so the
      Plates and the Masthead cannot pad it differently. */
  ordinal: number;
  title: string;
  href: string;
  spec: string;
  year: string | number;
}

const { ordinal, title, href, spec, year } = Astro.props;

/* Moved here with the markup it formats. It was a page-level helper in
   index.astro when the Plates were its only caller. */
const pad = (n: number) => String(n).padStart(2, '0');
---

{/* ALWAYS IN THE DOM, hidden only by opacity: a screen reader reads a complete
    Plate, a crawler indexes one, and the document without CSS is the list it has
    always been. Never `display: none` and never `hidden`. */}
<div class="meta">
  <p class="t-spec ordinal">{pad(ordinal)}</p>

  {/* THE <span> IS LOAD-BEARING AND THE BUG IT PREVENTS IS SILENT. The rise is a
      transform, a transform makes its element the containing block for
      positioned descendants, and `.title a::after` is the stretched hit area
      covering the whole Plate — or the whole portrait. Transform the <a> and
      that `inset: 0` collapses onto the title: the reveal still looks right and
      most of the image stops being clickable. The span carries the motion so the
      anchor never has to. */}
  <h2 class="title">
    <a href={href}><span>{title}</span></a>
  </h2>

  {/* The Specification Line. `credit` is deliberately NOT here —
      content-architecture.md §2 puts provenance on the detail page's fact block,
      and an index that states where every piece was made is an index about
      employment. */}
  <p class="t-spec spec">
    {spec} <span class="year">{year}</span>
  </p>
</div>

<style>
  /* THE METADATA BLOCK. In flow here, under the image, which is what it is on a
     phone and under any coarse pointer. It only becomes an overlay in the wide
     mode below.

     --scrim IS DECLARED ON IT, and the move is what makes this component
     correct rather than merely tidy. It used to sit on index.astro's `main` and
     reach both bands by inheritance, and its own comment authorised exactly this
     with the trigger "if a third route ever reveals a label on an image it gets
     promoted, and the derivation moves with it". The trigger fired — the band is
     on the Project pages and /404 now — but the promotion that comment expected
     was to tokens.css, and that is the wrong target: componentising collapsed
     two consumers on one page into one consumer in one file, and a token exists
     to stop two places writing one value. base.css sets the same precedent for
     --rise and the --reveal-* trio, which are on :root beside the rules that
     read them rather than in tokens.css.

     LEAVING IT BEHIND WOULD HAVE FAILED SILENTLY, which is the reason this
     paragraph exists. On a Project page `var(--scrim)` would resolve to the
     guaranteed-invalid value, `background` would be dropped, and --ink text
     would paint over an unmodified render. Neither instrument can see that:
     check-css.mjs's contrast pass harvests :root and compares tokens to
     --ground, and Lighthouse does not evaluate contrast over an image at all.

     THE 0.94 IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A TASTE. ADR-0008 originally refused a
     gradient here on the grounds that one cannot promise 4.5:1 over arbitrary
     pixels. That is true of an unbounded gradient; this one is bounded, and the
     bound is what makes it safe:

       A black scrim at alpha over a white pixel composites to 255(1 - alpha),
       and the text needs 4.5:1 against that. For --muted (luminance 0.198,
       which is the 4.96:1 check-css.mjs reports on the Ground) the backdrop
       must sit at or below (0.198 + 0.05) / 4.5 - 0.05 = 0.0051, a channel
       value of about 16, so alpha >= 0.938. For --ink (luminance 0.792) the
       same arithmetic gives alpha >= 0.595.

     THAT GAP IS THE WHOLE DESIGN, and it took two rounds of owner review to see
     it. The band began as an opaque --ground block, then as a fade above a 0.97
     plateau, and both came back as "too much black". The instruction that
     resolved it was "start the fade right from the bottom of the frame instead
     of starting above the text".

     A FADE THAT STARTS AT THE BOTTOM CANNOT CARRY --muted, and this is
     arithmetic rather than preference. Continuous means alpha is ALREADY falling
     where the text sits, so the binding point is the TOP of the text rather than
     the bottom of the band. --muted needs 0.94 there, which is another way of
     writing "no fade through the text" — the plateau was not a cautious choice,
     it was the only shape --muted admits. Measured at the text's top edge:

       alpha    --muted        --ink
       0.66     1.65 FAIL      5.79 ok
       0.70     2.01 FAIL      6.83 ok
       0.94     4.52 ok        15.34 ok

     SO THE BAND'S SMALL TEXT IS --ink, and that is a real cost paid
     deliberately. `.spec` in the Masthead still refuses exactly this override,
     and it is right to: on the Ground, colour is a free choice and a spec line
     that reads one way beside another spec line is not a style. OVER AN IMAGE IT
     IS NOT A FREE CHOICE. The text colour is the only thing setting how much
     render can show through, because it sets the alpha floor. --muted buys a
     slab; --ink buys a fade.

     0.66 AT THE TEXT'S TOP, NOT 0.595. Same reasoning ADR-0003 applies to
     --muted: pin for margin, not for the floor. 0.66 measures 5.79 against a
     white pixel, so a render would have to be brighter than white to trouble it,
     and the scrim is light enough that the image reads through the text.

     IT REACHES 1 AT THE BOTTOM EDGE, AND THAT IS A JOIN RATHER THAN A SLAB. The
     stop before this one bottomed out at 0.85, which left the image faintly
     visible along the frame's last few pixels and then cut hard to the page
     beneath — a seam exactly one Plate-edge wide. --ground is #000000, so an
     opaque foot IS the page: the band stops having a bottom edge and the frame
     dissolves into the ground instead of ending on it. The foot is 10% of the
     band, ~28px, and it sits under the specification line rather than behind the
     block.

     SEVEN STOPS AND NO PLATEAU ABOVE THE FOOT. Alpha falls continuously from 10%
     to the top and never holds, which is what makes the band read as the image
     receding rather than as a panel laid on it. Retune a band's height and
     re-check the alpha at the text's TOP edge, by hand — CI cannot. The way to
     check it is to force the bands visible, hide their text so only the scrim is
     painted, screenshot the text zone and take the brightest pixel in it.

     HIDE THE DEV TOOLBAR BEFORE MEASURING. It paints a rgb(66,68,72) pill over
     the bottom centre of the viewport, which lands inside three of the six
     bands and reports 2.30. That is the toolbar, not the page. */
  .meta {
    margin: var(--s3) 0 0;

    --scrim: linear-gradient(
      to top,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 1) 0%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0.98) 10%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0.88) 25%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0.68) 45%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0.45) 62%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2) 80%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 0) 100%
    );
  }

  /* `z-index: 1` IS LOAD-BEARING AND THE BUG IT FIXES IS SILENT, which is the
     same sentence `.title a::after` carries and a different bug.

     In the Masthead above 1200px the <picture> becomes `position: absolute` so
     the face can take the prose's height. A positioned element paints above a
     non-positioned in-flow one regardless of DOM order, so without this the band
     renders UNDERNEATH the portrait: the reveal fires, the opacity changes, and
     nothing appears. Caught in a browser, because it looks exactly like a rule
     that never matched.

     It applies in both modes rather than being scoped to the branch that needs
     it. A z-index that only exists on one side of a breakpoint is a bug waiting
     for the other side to grow a positioned child. */
  :global(.masthead-aside) .meta {
    z-index: 1;
  }

  .ordinal {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* THE INDEX MARK, in ink. It was signal red, and the change is a direction
     call rather than a compliance one: the site is greyscale everywhere the
     images are not, so that the eye goes to the work instead of to the furniture
     around it.

     WHAT THIS LEAVES BEHIND: --signal is used by exactly one selector on the
     whole site, the :focus-visible outline in base.css. The token stays and its
     3:1 floor in scripts/check-css.mjs stays with it.

     A background, never a colour: --signal fails 4.5:1 as text and check-css.mjs
     refuses it in a `color:` declaration by name.

     26px AND 2px ARE NOT SPACING, which is why the check in check-css.mjs is
     scoped to margin, padding and gap rather than to every length. This is the
     size of a drawn mark. */
  .ordinal::before {
    content: '';
    display: block;
    width: 26px;
    height: 2px;
    margin-bottom: var(--s2);
    background: var(--ink);
  }

  .title {
    margin: var(--s2) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  /* One link per band, and it covers the whole image. The <a> is on the title so
     its accessible name is the Project's name — or `About` — and the
     pseudo-element extends the hit area over the image without adding a second
     link to the same URL for a screen reader to read out twice.

     WRAPPING THE <picture> INSTEAD WOULD FOLD THE ALT TEXT INTO THE LINK'S NAME.
     On the Masthead that link would announce as "Coulter Heiberger, smiling, in
     glasses and a light blue collared shirt against a warm stucco wall. About".
     This way there is one link named `About` and one image keeping its own
     description.

     `z-index: 1` IS LOAD-BEARING AND THE BUG IT FIXES IS SILENT. Without it an
     overlay can sit under the image and the image takes the click, so the single
     largest, most obviously clickable thing on the page does nothing. Confirmed
     once with elementsFromPoint over the image centre, which returned the
     overlay present, and under.

     The cause was painting order rather than geometry: grid items paint as
     atomic units in document order, so a later sibling paints over an earlier
     sibling's positioned descendant. Here the <picture> comes FIRST and this
     overlay follows it, which is the arrangement that does not trip it — and the
     z-index is kept anyway, because it is one declaration against a bug that
     costs a browser session to rediscover. Do not "fix" a recurrence by
     reordering the DOM; that trades a silent mouse bug for a reading-order one.

     IT IS ALSO THE BAND'S ONLY FOCUSABLE THING, which is what `:focus-within`
     below hangs on.

     THE `inset: 0` RESOLVES AGAINST THE CONSUMER, NOT AGAINST THIS COMPONENT.
     `.meta` is deliberately never positioned — see the note on the wide-mode
     geometry — so the nearest positioned ancestor is `.plate` or
     `.masthead-aside`, both of which carry `position: relative`. A consumer that
     renders this band without one gets a hit area stretched to the viewport. */
  .title a {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .title a::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  .spec {
    margin: var(--s3) 0 0;
  }

  /* The year rides the specification line rather than sitting under it: over an
     image it is one more line to reserve height for, and the two are the same
     voice at the same size saying two halves of one fact.

     THE SEPARATOR IS SPACE, NOT THE MIDDLE DOT. The dot separates tools INSIDE a
     specification line, and reusing it here would make the year read as one more
     tool. */
  .year::before {
    content: '';
    display: inline-block;
    width: var(--s4);
  }

  /* ---- wide mode ---------------------------------------------------------
     Same condition as the Plates, the crops, and tokens.css's --gap switch: a
     SHAPE, not a width. See WIDE_MODE in src/layout.ts. */
  @media (min-aspect-ratio: 1 / 1) {
    /* THE BAND. Fixed height so all six Plates read as one row of the same
       object rather than as six blocks sized by how long their titles are, and
       bottom aligned inside it so the datum sits on the image's own edge.

       IT WAS AN OPAQUE `--ground` BLOCK SIZED BY ITS CONTENT, filling the bottom
       ~30% of the image, which meant pointing at the work hid the work. ADR-0008
       is amended with the measurement that makes the scrim safe; the derivation
       is on --scrim above.

       280px IS A DRAWN DIMENSION, not spacing, which is why check-css.mjs's
       refusal is scoped to margin, padding and gap rather than to every length —
       the same category as the ordinal's 26px rule.

       IT GOT TALLER WHILE THE BLACK GOT SMALLER, which sounds contradictory and
       is the whole adjustment. The band's height is the length the gradient has
       to fall off over, NOT the amount of ground painted: what a visitor reads
       as "black" is the region near full strength, and that is set by the text
       plus its padding rather than by this number. Going 260 -> 280 bought 47px
       more fade; dropping the vertical padding to --s3 took 27px off the slab.
       Shorten the band and the falloff gets steeper, not lighter.

       PADDING IS --s3 VERTICAL, --s4 HORIZONTAL. The horizontal value holds the
       text's inset from the image edge; the vertical one sets how far up the
       gradient the text's top edge reaches, which is the number the contrast
       check turns on. The text spans 7% to 45% of the band, and the scrim is at
       0.66 by 45%, worth 5.79:1 to --ink.

       THERE IS NO PLATEAU LEFT TO SHRINK. Raise this padding or shorten the band
       and the text's top edge climbs into thinner scrim — re-check it against
       the table on --scrim rather than assuming, because nothing in CI will say
       so.

       NEVER `position: absolute` ON THIS BLOCK, and that is the fix for a bug
       that shipped: CLICKING A PLATE'S IMAGE DID NOTHING. Positioned, `.meta`
       becomes the containing block for `.title a::after`, so its `inset: 0`
       resolved to the 169px band instead of to the 570px Plate. Measured before
       the fix: `a.offsetParent === .meta` returned true. Grid stacking removes
       the cause rather than patching the symptom — `.meta` is a grid item and
       not positioned, so the nearest positioned ancestor is the consumer again.
       If the band ever needs to escape its cell, move the overlay off
       `.title a` first.

       THE ROW STILL RESOLVES TO THE IMAGE'S HEIGHT, so revealing the band cannot
       move anything. That was the reason for absolute positioning and it
       survives: the band is 280px against a hero of ~510px, the picture is the
       taller item, and it sizes the row. */
    /* THE TWO CONSUMERS GATE IT DIFFERENTLY, and that is the one thing they do
       not share. The Masthead's portrait is a 200px square below 1200px — a
       280px band on it is a black tile with a face behind it — so its band waits
       for the branch where the column stretches to the prose beside it. A
       Plate's image is full width in this mode and needs no second condition.
       Everything the two bands genuinely share is unscoped. */
    :global(.plate) .meta {
      grid-area: 1 / 1;
      align-self: end;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      justify-content: flex-end;
      height: 280px;
      margin: 0;
      padding: var(--s3) var(--s4);
      background: var(--scrim);
      opacity: 0;
    }

    /* --ink, NOT .t-spec's --muted, on the ordinal and the specification line.
       The full argument is on --scrim: a scrim that fades from the bottom edge
       cannot hold --muted at AA, so over an image the text colour is what
       decides how much render shows through. The title is an h2 at --t-h3 and is
       already --ink, so this brings the other two lines to the colour the title
       has always had in this band rather than introducing a new one.

       UNSCOPED, unlike the geometry above. Both bands paint small text over a
       render and the alpha arithmetic that forces --ink is the same arithmetic
       in both. */
    .meta .t-spec {
      color: var(--ink);
    }

    /* THE SITE'S TWO `:hover` RULES (ADR-0008, docs/motion.md) ARE NOW ONE RULE
       SERVING BOTH OBJECTS, which is what the extraction bought. They were a
       Plate's band and the Masthead portrait's `About` label, held to identical
       terms by hand in one file. The hovered SELECTORS still cannot be shared —
       one is `.plate`, one is `.masthead-aside` — so those are listed; every
       term they act on is single-sourced below.

       IT IS TIMED (ADR-0009). The scrim fades over --reveal-in, the three lines
       rise --rise into it one --reveal-stagger behind, and all of it is gated on
       `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference` in the block below.

       THE DECLARATIONS HERE STAY UNTIMED, and that is the point of the split.
       This pair is still the whole reveal on a coarse pointer and under
       `reduce`. The motion is a branch off it, never a rewrite of it.

       `:focus-within` IS NOT A COURTESY. The overlay <a> is the only focusable
       thing in either object, so this is how a keyboard visitor reads the same
       specification line a pointer visitor does. Without it the metadata would
       be reachable by a mouse and by a screen reader and by nobody else.

       (hover: hover) GUARDS BOTH STATES TOGETHER. A coarse pointer never enters
       the hidden state at all, which is the point: guarding only the `:hover`
       half is the silent version of this bug, leaving the block at opacity 0 on
       a phone with no way to ever reveal it — on the Masthead, that is the one
       control leading to /about/ime/. */
    @media (hover: hover) {
      :global(.plate:hover) .meta,
      :global(.plate:focus-within) .meta {
        opacity: 1;
      }
    }

    @media not (hover: hover) {
      .meta {
        opacity: 1;
      }
    }

    /* THE MOTION. The scrim fades in place and the text rises into it, rather
       than the whole band translating: --scrim's top edge is a falloff rather
       than an edge, so translating it reads as a panel sliding in.

       ONE OPACITY, NOT TWO. `.meta`'s opacity fades the scrim AND the three
       lines inside it, which is the whole of "the text and the background fade".
       A second opacity on the lines would multiply two curves for nothing;
       --reveal-stagger is what separates them.

       THE THREE LINES ARE ONE ARRIVAL, so they share a delay rather than being
       staggered against each other. 60ms x 3 down a band this size would be the
       removed `rise` entrance rebuilt inside a hover, which is a different
       decision and not this one.

       WHY `.title a span` AND NOT `.title`, which is the trap this whole block
       is arranged around: `.title a::after` is the whole-image hit area,
       stretched with `inset: 0` against the consumer. A transform on ANY
       ancestor of that pseudo-element makes the ancestor its containing block,
       and the hit area collapses out of the image and onto the band. The object
       goes on revealing perfectly and stops being clickable above the metadata.

       AND NO, SETTING THE HOVERED STATE TO `transform: none` IS NOT ENOUGH ON
       ITS OWN. Mid-transition the computed transform is not none, so the naive
       version has a --reveal-in-long dead zone over the render on every pointer
       entry — long enough for a fast click to land in it. The span keeps the
       anchor out of the transformed subtree entirely.

       A BRANCH OFF THE SWAP ABOVE, NEVER A REPLACEMENT FOR IT. The opacity
       endpoints are not restated in here: move them in and a visitor who asked
       their OS for less motion loses the reveal entirely, which is the silent
       version of this. */
    @media (hover: hover) and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      .meta {
        transition: opacity var(--reveal-out) ease-out;
      }

      /* `display: inline-block` ON THE SPAN ONLY, and it is required rather than
         tidy: `transform` does not apply to a non-replaced inline box, and a
         <span> in an <a> is one. Without it the two <p> lines rise and the title
         does not, which looks like a typo in the selector list. The <p>s are
         already block boxes and must not be given it — inline-block would take
         them off the flex column's full width. */
      .title a span {
        display: inline-block;
      }

      /* THE RISE IS SCOPED TO THE CONSUMERS, and it has to be. Both bands use
         these three classes, but each is only an overlay inside its own gate —
         the Masthead's above 1200px, a Plate's in this mode. Where the block
         sits in flow it is visible with no reveal to rise out of, so unscoped
         these lines would hold an 8px offset that nothing ever resets, which
         reads as a spacing bug on a window 1100px wide. */
      :global(.plate) .ordinal,
      :global(.plate) .title a span,
      :global(.plate) .spec {
        transform: translateY(var(--rise));
        transition: transform var(--reveal-out) ease-out;
      }

      :global(.plate:hover) .meta,
      :global(.plate:focus-within) .meta {
        transition-duration: var(--reveal-in);
      }

      /* `transform: none`, not `translateY(0)`. A specified transform of zero
         still establishes a containing block; `none` does not, so at rest
         `.title a::after` resolves against the consumer exactly as it did before
         this block existed. The <span> in the markup makes this belt-and-braces
         rather than the fix, and it stays for that reason. */
      :global(.plate:hover) .ordinal,
      :global(.plate:hover) .title a span,
      :global(.plate:hover) .spec,
      :global(.plate:focus-within) .ordinal,
      :global(.plate:focus-within) .title a span,
      :global(.plate:focus-within) .spec {
        transform: none;
        transition-duration: var(--reveal-in);
        transition-delay: var(--reveal-stagger);
      }
    }

    /* NO FONT-SIZE HERE, and that is deliberate. The obvious move over a render
       is to drop the title to the mono datum voice, and it would have been a
       fourth type treatment — the exact mechanism base.css records for how this
       site once reached ten treatments. The title is an h2 at --t-h3 over an
       image the same way it is one beside an image.

       THE TIGHTER SPEC MARGIN, --s1 RATHER THAN --s3. It used to need restating
       for the Masthead's band, because `.masthead .spec` at (0,2,0) beat this
       bare `.spec` inside the header and left the About band at the wide 20px
       while the six below it sat at 8px. The Masthead is a separate component
       now, so its scope attribute and this one do not see each other and the
       restatement is gone. */
    .spec {
      margin: var(--s1) 0 0;
    }
  }

  /* ---- the Masthead's band, and its second condition is the point ----------
     BOTH CONDITIONS, not just the shape. The mode switch is an ASPECT RATIO, so
     an 844x390 landscape phone reaches the wide branch with the portrait still
     at its 200px square — and a 280px band on a 200px face is a black tile with
     a face behind it. 1200px is where that column stops being a fixed square and
     stretches to the prose beside it, which is the condition the band actually
     needs. Same boundary and same reasoning as the two Masthead rules that set
     up the stretch.

     THE HEIGHT GATE IS ARITHMETIC, not caution. The scrim's contrast bound puts
     the text's TOP edge at or below 45% of the band, and this content is ~110px
     tall, so the band cannot be much under 280px without pushing the small text
     into thinner scrim. 280px over a 200px square would be taller than the face.

     BELOW THIS THE BLOCK SITS IN FLOW under the portrait, visible, exactly as a
     Plate's metadata does on a phone. EVERY hover and motion rule for this
     consumer is inside this gate for that reason: in flow there is no reveal to
     rise out of, so an unscoped rise would hold an 8px offset that nothing ever
     resets, which reads as a spacing bug on a window 1100px wide. */
  @media (min-aspect-ratio: 1 / 1) and (min-width: 1200px) {
    /* Every number here is the Plates'. Same height, same padding, same scrim,
       same bottom alignment — if one of them moves, both move, or the two
       objects stop being one device. They are restated rather than shared
       because the two gates differ, which is the one genuine difference between
       the consumers. */
    :global(.masthead-aside) .meta {
      grid-area: 1 / 1;
      align-self: end;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      justify-content: flex-end;
      height: 280px;
      margin: 0;
      padding: var(--s3) var(--s4);
      background: var(--scrim);
      opacity: 0;
    }

    /* Both states guarded together, for the reason the Plates' copy gives:
       guarding only the `:hover` half leaves the band at opacity 0 on a coarse
       pointer with no way to ever reveal it — and this is the one control on the
       page leading to /about/ime/.

       `:focus-within` IS NOT A COURTESY. The title's <a> is the only focusable
       thing in this column, so without it a keyboard visitor would tab onto an
       invisible link. */
    @media (hover: hover) {
      :global(.masthead-aside:hover) .meta,
      :global(.masthead-aside:focus-within) .meta {
        opacity: 1;
      }
    }

    @media not (hover: hover) {
      :global(.masthead-aside) .meta {
        opacity: 1;
      }
    }

    /* A BRANCH OFF THE SWAP ABOVE, NEVER A REPLACEMENT FOR IT. The opacity
       endpoints are not restated in here: move them in and a visitor who asked
       their OS for less motion loses the reveal entirely. */
    @media (hover: hover) and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      :global(.masthead-aside) .meta {
